    "body": "## What\n\nExpand the docstring of `BlendedMegatronDatasetConfig.mmap_bin_files` to call out that setting it to `False` is the right knob for **parallel-filesystem mmap-quota exhaustion**, not only for S3 / object storage.\n\nCurrent docstring (one line):\n\n> Whether to mmap the .bin files or use file pointers.\n\nNew docstring lists the two known cases where users should disable mmap:\n\n1. Object storage backends (S3, etc.).\n2. Parallel FS (Lustre, WekaFS, ...) when a blend contains many prefixes — the FS client can enforce a per-process live-mmap quota lower than `vm.max_map_count` and raise `PermissionError: [Errno 1] Operation not permitted` (EPERM) from `numpy.memmap` partway through `IndexedDataset` construction.\n\n## Why\n\nDocs-only. Came out of [NVIDIA-NeMo/Megatron-Bridge#3968](https://github.com/NVIDIA-NeMo/Megatron-Bridge/issues/3968), where a 444-prefix × {train, valid} = 888-dataset blend on a WekaFS mount reproducibly EPERM'd at `indexed_dataset.py:280` after ~590 successful builds — single-rank, no concurrency.\n\nThe reporter spent real time ruling out three other \"DataLoader init speedup\" flags (`path_to_cache`, `defer_npy_index_mmap`, `fast_cache_load`) — none of those reduce the per-prefix live-mmap count, which is what the FS client is actually sensitive to. Only `mmap_bin_files=False` works, because it swaps `_MMapBinReader` for `_FileBinReader` and halves the per-dataset mmap count (the `.idx` mmap in `_IndexReader.__init__` is still unconditional).\n\nThe current docstring gives users no hint that this is a possibility on parallel FSes, so they go down the wrong rabbit holes. This is the cheapest possible mitigation: document the workaround so the next person finds it from the config.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Making the `.idx` mmap optional (would need a new flag and is a bigger change — could be a follow-up).\n- Auto-detecting parallel FS and flipping the default.\n- Working with Weka on a client-side fix.\n\n## Test\n\nDocs-only, no code path changes. CI should be a no-op.",
